First, I know that if a product of two expressions equals zero, then at least one of the expressions must be zero. So, the solutions to the equation are the solutions to 3x² + kx + 3 = 0 and the solutions to x² + kx - 1 = 0. Therefore, the total number of distinct real roots will be the number of distinct real roots of the first quadratic plus the number of distinct real roots of the second quadratic, minus any overlaps where roots are common to both quadratics.

So, my task is to determine how many distinct real roots each quadratic can have, depending on k, and then figure out the minimum total number considering possible overlaps. Since we want the minimum number of distinct real roots, I need to consider cases where the quadratics have as few roots as possible and where they might share roots (so overlapping roots would reduce the total count).

